<td id="S1.T1.13.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S1.T1.13.5.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">learned scalar</span></td>
<td id="S1.T1.13.5.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S1.T1.13.5.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">–</span></td>
<td id="S1.T1.13.5.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S1.T1.13.5.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">75</a>, <a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">60</a>, <a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">70</a><span id="S1.T1.13.5.5.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">]</span></cite></td></tr>
<tr id="S1.T1.13.6" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S1.T1.13.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S1.T1.13.6.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Recurrence-side</span></td>
<td id="S1.T1.13.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S1.T1.13.6.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">–</span></td>
<td id="S1.T1.13.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S1.T1.13.6.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">varies</span></td>
<td id="S1.T1.13.6.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S1.T1.13.6.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">varies</span></td>
<td id="S1.T1.13.6.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S1.T1.13.6.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">[</span><a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">68</a>, <a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>, <a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a><span id="S1.T1.13.6.5.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">]</span></cite></td></tr>
<tr id="S1.T1.13.7" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S1.T1.13.7.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S1.T1.13.7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">Graph routing (ours)</span></td>
<td id="S1.T1.13.7.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S1.T1.13.7.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">raster</span></td>
<td id="S1.T1.13.7.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S1.T1.13.7.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">learned affinities</span></td>
<td id="S1.T1.13.7.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S1.T1.13.7.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">semantic window</span></td>
<td id="S1.T1.13.7.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S1.T1.13.7.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">GraphScan</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Despite this progress, most adaptive scans remain primarily geometric: coordinate-offset methods sample at predicted positions through bilinear interpolation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>, <a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>, score- and sort-based methods reorder tokens without aggregating local semantic neighborhoods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">75</a>, <a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>, and recurrence-side methods modify the SSM mixer itself rather than the visual tokens entering it <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">68</a>, <a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>]</cite>. This leaves open a simple possibility: before asking an SSM to propagate information globally, can we first make each token locally and semantically aware?</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We answer this question with <span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GraphScan</span>, a graph-induced dynamic scanning operator for Vision Mamba. Instead of predicting a continuous sampling coordinate, GraphScan constructs a spatially bounded local graph around each token and learns feature-conditioned affinities with relative positional bias.
Each output token is produced by one-step message passing over this semantic neighborhood and then passed to the selective SSM for global aggregation.
GraphScan therefore does not replace the SSM with a graph neural network <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">58</a>, <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>; rather, it uses graph-based routing as a lightweight scan-time operator that prepares locally grounded tokens for the global sequence mixer.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">This gives GraphScan a distinct role among existing scan mechanisms: where fixed scans choose a path, deformable scans choose coordinates, and score-based scans choose an ordering, GraphScan chooses a local semantic neighborhood. The distinction matters because the selective SSM compresses visual context along a one-dimensional processing path; if the tokens entering it already encode spatially nearby and semantically relevant neighbors, the global mixer receives a better-conditioned sequence without changing its recurrence. GraphScan is therefore an input-side complement to recurrence-side advances such as SSD, non-causal SSD, and newer Mamba variants <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>, <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">We instantiate GraphScan in a hierarchical Vision Mamba backbone, <span id="S1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GraphScan-Mamba</span>, on the Mamba-1/S6 selective-scan interface used by the dominant scan-mechanism literature <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">78</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>, <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">72</a>, <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>, <a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>, <a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">68</a>, <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>, <a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>, enabling direct comparison with fixed, local, multi-scale, deformable, and dynamic scan baselines. GraphScan is orthogonal to the choice of SSM core: it changes the tokens presented to the mixer, not the mixer dynamics.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate GraphScan-Mamba under standard vision-backbone protocols (ImageNet-1K classification, COCO detection/instance segmentation with Mask R-CNN, ADE20K segmentation with UperNet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>, <a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">76</a>, <a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">69</a>]</cite>), where it achieves state-of-the-art performance among Vision SSM backbones at comparable parameter and FLOP budgets. Ablations isolate the role of semantic routing, neighborhood size, relative positional bias, and pre-SSM insertion, and visualizations confirm that GraphScan induces interpretable displacement fields over the token lattice. Together, these results suggest a new design principle: scanning should not be treated as geometric serialization but as learned semantic routing before global state-space modeling.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Vision Backbones and SSMs</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Vision backbones span convolutional <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">65</a>, <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">74</a>]</cite>, Transformer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">55</a>, <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>, <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">77</a>]</cite>, MLP-mixing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>]</cite>, and graph-based <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">66</a>]</cite> families; we refer to App. <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Extended Related Work ‣ Can Graphs Help Vision SSMs See Better?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a> for a fuller treatment. State-space sequence models provide efficient long-range mixing with near-linear scaling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>, <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite>, and Mamba introduced selective state spaces with input-dependent recurrence parameters <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>, later extended through structured state space duality and recurrence-level improvements <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>. Our work addresses a complementary question: how should a two-dimensional visual feature map be routed before it is processed by a one-dimensional selective scan?</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Vision Mamba Scans and Dynamic Routing</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Vision Mamba models convert feature maps to token sequences using fixed scan patterns—bidirectional, two-dimensional, continuous, local-window, atrous, multi-scale, grouped, or space-filling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">78</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>, <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">72</a>, <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>, <a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>, <a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>, <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>, <a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>]</cite>. Adaptive variants reroute tokens before the SSM through learned partitions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">71</a>]</cite>, predicted continuous offsets with bilinear interpolation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>, <a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>, content-dependent reordering <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">75</a>, <a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">60</a>, <a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>, dynamic state aggregation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">70</a>]</cite>, structure-aware state fusion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">68</a>]</cite>, non-causal SSD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>]</cite>, or Mamba–Transformer hybridization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>. GraphScan instead performs one-step semantic message passing on a local graph over visual tokens before the SSM: input-adaptive like dynamic scans, spatially bounded like local scans, and semantic like graph aggregation, while preserving token count and linear scaling.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Method</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">GraphScan is designed around a simple principle: before a one-dimensional selective SSM performs global sequence mixing, each visual token should first be enriched by the local semantic neighborhood from which it arises. In this section, we first review the Vision Mamba selective-scan interface, then reinterpret adaptive scanning as pre-SSM token routing, introduce the GraphScan operator, and finally describe its integration into the GraphScan-Mamba backbone.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Vision Selective Scan Preliminaries</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\in\mathbb{R}^{H\times W\times D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\in\mathbb{R}^{H\times W\times D}</annotation></semantics></math> denote an intermediate visual feature map with <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> channels. We flatten it into a token sequence <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}=[\mathbf{x}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{x}_{L}]\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}=[\mathbf{x}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{x}_{L}]\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times D}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=HW" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=HW</annotation></semantics></math>. Each token <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> has a discrete lattice coordinate <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{g}_{i}\in\{1,\ldots,H\}\times\{1,\ldots,W\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{g}_{i}\in\{1,\ldots,H\}\times\{1,\ldots,W\}</annotation></semantics></math> and a normalized coordinate <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}_{i}\in[-1,1]^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}_{i}\in[-1,1]^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. The downstream SSM consumes the flattened sequence in raster order unless otherwise specified. Batch dimensions are omitted for clarity.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Selective SSM interface.</span>
A Mamba-style selective SSM maps an input sequence <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}=[\mathbf{u}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{u}_{L}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐔</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}=[\mathbf{u}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{u}_{L}]</annotation></semantics></math> to an output sequence through an input-dependent recurrence <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>. Let <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the SSM state dimension. For the Mamba-1/S6 interface used by most Vision Mamba backbones, each channel maintains an <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional recurrent state. At position <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, the static transition parameter <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}\in\mathbb{R}^{D\times N_{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}\in\mathbb{R}^{D\times N_{s}}</annotation></semantics></math> is combined with input-dependent quantities generated from <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>: a final positive step size <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\Delta}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝚫</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\Delta}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{D}</annotation></semantics></math> and selective vectors <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{b}_{t},\mathbf{c}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐛</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐜</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{b}_{t},\mathbf{c}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{s}}</annotation></semantics></math>. After discretization, this yields effective tensors <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathbf{A}}_{t},\bar{\mathbf{B}}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{D\times N_{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐁</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathbf{A}}_{t},\bar{\mathbf{B}}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{D\times N_{s}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathbf{A}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathbf{A}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> controls state retention and <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathbf{B}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐁</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathbf{B}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> controls the input write into the state. The recurrent state is <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{H}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{D\times N_{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐇</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{H}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{D\times N_{s}}</annotation></semantics></math>. We use two broadcast operators. For <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{D}</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{u}_{t}^{\uparrow_{s}}\in\mathbb{R}^{D\times N_{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐮</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}_{t}^{\uparrow_{s}}\in\mathbb{R}^{D\times N_{s}}</annotation></semantics></math> denote broadcasting over the state dimension, i.e., <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m15" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="[\mathbf{u}_{t}^{\uparrow_{s}}]_{d,n}=\mathbf{u}_{t,d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\mathbf{u}_{t}^{\uparrow_{s}}]_{d,n}=\mathbf{u}_{t,d}</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{c}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐜</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{c}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{s}}</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m17" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{c}_{t}^{\uparrow_{D}}\in\mathbb{R}^{D\times N_{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐜</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>D</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{c}_{t}^{\uparrow_{D}}\in\mathbb{R}^{D\times N_{s}}</annotation></semantics></math> denote broadcasting over the channel dimension, i.e., <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m18" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="[\mathbf{c}_{t}^{\uparrow_{D}}]_{d,n}=\mathbf{c}_{t,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐜</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>D</mi></msub></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝐜</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\mathbf{c}_{t}^{\uparrow_{D}}]_{d,n}=\mathbf{c}_{t,n}</annotation></semantics></math>. The core update is</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{H}_{t}=\bar{\mathbf{A}}_{t}\odot\mathbf{H}_{t-1}+\bar{\mathbf{B}}_{t}\odot\mathbf{u}_{t}^{\uparrow_{s}},\qquad\mathbf{y}_{t}=\left\langle\mathbf{H}_{t},\mathbf{c}_{t}^{\uparrow_{D}}\right\rangle_{N_{s}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐇</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><msub><mi>𝐇</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐁</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></msubsup><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><msub><mi>𝐇</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐜</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>D</mi></msub></mrow></msubsup><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{H}_{t}=\bar{\mathbf{A}}_{t}\odot\mathbf{H}_{t-1}+\bar{\mathbf{B}}_{t}\odot\mathbf{u}_{t}^{\uparrow_{s}},\qquad\mathbf{y}_{t}=\left\langle\mathbf{H}_{t},\mathbf{c}_{t}^{\uparrow_{D}}\right\rangle_{N_{s}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle\cdot,\cdot\rangle_{N_{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle\cdot,\cdot\rangle_{N_{s}}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes contraction over the SSM state dimension, producing <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{D}</annotation></semantics></math>. Across the full sequence, the post-discretization tensors have shape <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathbf{A}},\bar{\mathbf{B}}\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times D\times N_{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐁</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathbf{A}},\bar{\mathbf{B}}\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times D\times N_{s}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the output sequence satisfies <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Y}\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Y}\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times D}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The key property for our work is selectivity: <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\Delta}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝚫</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\Delta}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{b}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐛</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{b}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{c}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐜</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{c}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> are generated from the current token <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>. Therefore, modifying the token sequence before the SSM changes both the values written into the recurrent state and the selective parameters that govern propagation and readout. Mamba-2 and later SSD-style models provide alternative formulations and more efficient algorithms for related state-space sequence transformations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>; our method targets the input sequence presented to the SSM and is therefore orthogonal to these recurrence-level developments.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Parallel scan computation.</span>
Although Eq. (<a href="#S3.E1" title="In 3.1 Vision Selective Scan Preliminaries ‣ 3 Method ‣ Can Graphs Help Vision SSMs See Better?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) is written recurrently, selective scan is computed efficiently using an associative prefix-scan operator <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>. Define the write term <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{U}_{t}=\bar{\mathbf{B}}_{t}\odot\mathbf{u}_{t}^{\uparrow_{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐔</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐁</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}_{t}=\bar{\mathbf{B}}_{t}\odot\mathbf{u}_{t}^{\uparrow_{s}}</annotation></semantics></math> and the affine update pair <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{P}_{t}=(\bar{\mathbf{A}}_{t},\mathbf{U}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐏</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐔</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{P}_{t}=(\bar{\mathbf{A}}_{t},\mathbf{U}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>. Composing two updates gives <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbf{A}_{2},\mathbf{U}_{2})\circ(\mathbf{A}_{1},\mathbf{U}_{1})=(\mathbf{A}_{2}\odot\mathbf{A}_{1},\;\mathbf{A}_{2}\odot\mathbf{U}_{1}+\mathbf{U}_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐔</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐔</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><msub><mi>𝐔</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>𝐔</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbf{A}_{2},\mathbf{U}_{2})\circ(\mathbf{A}_{1},\mathbf{U}_{1})=(\mathbf{A}_{2}\odot\mathbf{A}_{1},\;\mathbf{A}_{2}\odot\mathbf{U}_{1}+\mathbf{U}_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>, which is associative. Thus all hidden states can be obtained by a parallel prefix scan over <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathbf{P}_{t}\}_{t=1}^{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝐏</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathbf{P}_{t}\}_{t=1}^{L}</annotation></semantics></math>, preserving linear scaling in sequence length while avoiding materializing a dense <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\times L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\times L</annotation></semantics></math> mixing matrix.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Unrolled view.</span>
Unrolling Eq. (<a href="#S3.E1" title="In 3.1 Vision Selective Scan Preliminaries ‣ 3 Method ‣ Can Graphs Help Vision SSMs See Better?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) from a zero initial state yields <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{H}_{t}=\sum_{i=1}^{t}\mathbf{G}_{t,i}\odot\bar{\mathbf{B}}_{i}\odot\mathbf{u}_{i}^{\uparrow_{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐇</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mo>∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msup><mi>𝐆</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐁</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{H}_{t}=\sum_{i=1}^{t}\mathbf{G}_{t,i}\odot\bar{\mathbf{B}}_{i}\odot\mathbf{u}_{i}^{\uparrow_{s}}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{G}_{t,i}=\bigodot_{j=i+1}^{t}\bar{\mathbf{A}}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐆</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨀</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{G}_{t,i}=\bigodot_{j=i+1}^{t}\bar{\mathbf{A}}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> the selective survival factor from sequence position <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. With a nonzero initial state, an additional term <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{G}_{t,0}\odot\mathbf{H}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐆</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><msub><mi>𝐇</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{G}_{t,0}\odot\mathbf{H}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> appears, where <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{G}_{t,0}=\bigodot_{j=1}^{t}\bar{\mathbf{A}}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐆</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨀</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{G}_{t,0}=\bigodot_{j=1}^{t}\bar{\mathbf{A}}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>; for standard visual prefill over a feature map, we use the zero-state form for clarity. For visual features, this one-dimensional path is only an indirect representation of the original two-dimensional structure. A pair of patches can be adjacent in the image but distant in raster order, making the scan/routing operator a critical interface between visual geometry and SSM computation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>From Geometric Scanning to Semantic Routing</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Existing Vision Mamba backbones improve this interface by changing how the image is serialized or resampled before selective scan. Fixed scans choose a deterministic path through the lattice; local scans restrict processing to windows; adaptive coordinate-based scans predict new sampling locations before feeding features into the SSM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">78</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>, <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>, <a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>. Our goal is not to modify the SSM recurrence itself, but to improve the visual token sequence that enters it.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Geometric adaptive scan as local interpolation.</span>
Coordinate-based adaptive scans predict an offset <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\mathbf{p}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\mathbf{p}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for each reference position and sample from <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}^{\prime}_{i}=\mathbf{p}_{i}+\Delta\mathbf{p}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}^{\prime}_{i}=\mathbf{p}_{i}+\Delta\mathbf{p}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. The output token is obtained through bilinear interpolation, <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}^{\prime}_{i}=\sum_{j\in\mathcal{N}_{4}(\mathbf{p}^{\prime}_{i})}w^{\mathrm{bilin}}_{ij}\,\mathbf{x}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>bilin</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}^{\prime}_{i}=\sum_{j\in\mathcal{N}_{4}(\mathbf{p}^{\prime}_{i})}w^{\mathrm{bilin}}_{ij}\,\mathbf{x}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w^{\mathrm{bilin}}_{ij}\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>bilin</mi></msubsup><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w^{\mathrm{bilin}}_{ij}\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{j}w^{\mathrm{bilin}}_{ij}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>bilin</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{j}w^{\mathrm{bilin}}_{ij}=1</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}_{4}(\mathbf{p}^{\prime}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}_{4}(\mathbf{p}^{\prime}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the four lattice points surrounding the sampled coordinate. This is an effective form of input-dependent local rerouting, but the neighborhood and weights are determined by geometry: the predicted coordinate chooses the four corners and the interpolation rule fixes their weights.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Our view.</span>
We instead ask whether adaptive scanning can be made semantic. Rather than predicting where to sample in continuous coordinate space, GraphScan asks which nearby patches should communicate with the current token. It preserves the same interface as prior pre-SSM rerouting operators: the output remains a length-<math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> sequence in the original raster slots. However, the content assigned to each slot is produced by learned message passing over a local graph, with weights determined by inter-patch affinities.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>GraphScan: Local Semantic Routing</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">GraphScan constructs, for each token, a bounded local graph on the image lattice and performs one step of feature-conditioned message passing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib74" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>. It is deliberately lightweight: the neighborhood size is fixed by a small radius, the attention is local rather than global, and the output token count is unchanged. Fig. <a href="#S3.F2.fig1" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3.3 GraphScan: Local Semantic Routing ‣ 3 Method ‣ Can Graphs Help Vision SSMs See Better?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> illustrates the local affinity construction and message-passing update used to produce the routed token sequence.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:89.7pt;">
<div id="S3.F2.fig1.2" class="ltx_block ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:248.4pt;"><img src="2605.11300v1/GraphScan.png" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:343/97;" width="343" height="97" alt="Refer to caption">
</div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span><span id="S3.F2.fig1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">GraphScan.<span id="S3.F2.fig1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">
Each token attends to a bounded local neighborhood, aggregates semantically weighted neighboring features, and produces a routed token <math id="S3.F2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{\prime}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{\prime}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. The routed feature map <math id="S3.F2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is then passed to S6.
</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Local candidate set.</span>
For token <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, we define a spatial candidate set <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{r}(i)=\{j:\|\mathbf{g}_{j}-\mathbf{g}_{i}\|_{\infty}\leq r\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{r}(i)=\{j:\|\mathbf{g}_{j}-\mathbf{g}_{i}\|_{\infty}\leq r\}</annotation></semantics></math> of size <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=|\mathcal{S}_{r}(i)|=(2r+1)^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=|\mathcal{S}_{r}(i)|=(2r+1)^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> is the graph radius. In implementation, boundary tokens use replicate padding through coordinate clamping, so every token has the same <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> window slots. This avoids introducing artificial zero tokens into the softmax and keeps the operator shape-static.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Feature-conditioned affinities.</span>
For each token, GraphScan computes query, key, and value projections <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{q}_{i}=\mathbf{x}_{i}W_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{q}_{i}=\mathbf{x}_{i}W_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{k}_{j}=\mathbf{x}_{j}W_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐤</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{k}_{j}=\mathbf{x}_{j}W_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}_{j}=\mathbf{x}_{j}W_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}_{j}=\mathbf{x}_{j}W_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{q}_{i},\mathbf{k}_{j}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐤</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{q}_{i},\mathbf{k}_{j}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}_{j}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{v}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}_{j}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{v}}</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in\mathcal{S}_{r}(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in\mathcal{S}_{r}(i)</annotation></semantics></math>, the affinity score is</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{ij}=\frac{\mathbf{q}_{i}\mathbf{k}_{j}^{\top}}{\sqrt{d}}+b_{\mathrm{rel}}(\mathbf{g}_{j}-\mathbf{g}_{i}),\qquad\alpha_{ij}=\frac{\exp(s_{ij})}{\sum_{\ell\in\mathcal{S}_{r}(i)}\exp(s_{i\ell})}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐤</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow><msqrt><mi>d</mi></msqrt></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>rel</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐠</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{ij}=\frac{\mathbf{q}_{i}\mathbf{k}_{j}^{\top}}{\sqrt{d}}+b_{\mathrm{rel}}(\mathbf{g}_{j}-\mathbf{g}_{i}),\qquad\alpha_{ij}=\frac{\exp(s_{ij})}{\sum_{\ell\in\mathcal{S}_{r}(i)}\exp(s_{i\ell})}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">The learnable relative bias <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{\mathrm{rel}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>rel</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{\mathrm{rel}}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined over discrete offsets inside the local window. Unless otherwise stated, we use a single affinity head; a multi-head variant follows the standard head-splitting formulation and applies Eq. (<a href="#S3.E2" title="In 3.3 GraphScan: Local Semantic Routing ‣ 3 Method ‣ Can Graphs Help Vision SSMs See Better?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) independently per head, with a shared relative-position bias <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{\mathrm{rel}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>rel</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{\mathrm{rel}}</annotation></semantics></math> across heads to keep parameter shapes invariant under head count.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Message-passing update.</span>
The routed token is produced by aggregating value features from the learned semantic neighborhood:</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}^{\prime}_{i}=\mathbf{x}_{i}+\left(\sum_{j\in\mathcal{S}_{r}(i)}\alpha_{ij}\,\mathbf{v}_{j}\right)W_{o}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>o</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}^{\prime}_{i}=\mathbf{x}_{i}+\left(\sum_{j\in\mathcal{S}_{r}(i)}\alpha_{ij}\,\mathbf{v}_{j}\right)W_{o},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{o}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{v}\times D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{o}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{v}\times D}</annotation></semantics></math> projects the aggregated message back to the model dimension. Eq. (<a href="#S3.E3" title="In 3.3 GraphScan: Local Semantic Routing ‣ 3 Method ‣ Can Graphs Help Vision SSMs See Better?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) preserves the original token slot <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, but replaces an isolated patch representation with a semantic mixture of nearby visual evidence.
The local window specifies only where this evidence may come from; it is not a convolutional kernel.
While convolution uses content-independent weights tied to relative offsets, GraphScan recomputes token-specific weights from inter-patch affinities.
The operation is therefore related at the primitive level to local neighborhood attention and graph message passing over visual tokens <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib75" title="" class="ltx_ref">64</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">58</a>, <a href="#bib.bib74" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">66</a>]</cite>, but its architectural role is distinct.
GraphScan is not a standalone attention block or Vision-GNN backbone: it is a scan-time routing layer placed immediately before the selective SSM.
The routed tokens consequently affect both the values written into the recurrent state and the input-dependent parameters that govern state propagation and readout.
GraphScan therefore turns local semantic affinity into a preconditioning step for global state-space mixing (Sec. <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 GraphScan as Selective-Scan Preconditioning ‣ 3 Method ‣ Can Graphs Help Vision SSMs See Better?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>); a more detailed comparison to neighborhood attention and Vision-GNN primitives is given in Appendix <a href="#A1.SS7" title="A.7 Relation to Local Attention and Vision GNNs ‣ Appendix A Analysis of GraphScan-Induced Selective Scan ‣ Can Graphs Help Vision SSMs See Better?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.7</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Graph-induced scan field.</span>
For analysis and visualization, GraphScan induces an effective routed coordinate by taking the expected source position under the learned affinities, <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\mathbf{p}}_{i}=\sum_{j\in\mathcal{S}_{r}(i)}\alpha_{ij}\mathbf{p}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐩</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\mathbf{p}}_{i}=\sum_{j\in\mathcal{S}_{r}(i)}\alpha_{ij}\mathbf{p}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, with displacement <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\widehat{\mathbf{p}}_{i}=\widehat{\mathbf{p}}_{i}-\mathbf{p}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐩</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐩</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\widehat{\mathbf{p}}_{i}=\widehat{\mathbf{p}}_{i}-\mathbf{p}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since the weights <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> are nonnegative and sum to one, <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\mathbf{p}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐩</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\mathbf{p}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> remains inside the image coordinate domain whenever all <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}_{j}\in[-1,1]^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}_{j}\in[-1,1]^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. This yields a displacement-field visualization analogous to offset-based scans (Fig. <a href="#A4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ Appendix D Visualizations ‣ Can Graphs Help Vision SSMs See Better?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> in App. <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Visualizations ‣ Can Graphs Help Vision SSMs See Better?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>), but the displacement is induced by semantic routing rather than by direct coordinate regression.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Complexity.</span>
With fixed radius <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>, GraphScan has constant graph degree <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=(2r+1)^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=(2r+1)^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. The local affinity and aggregation cost scales as <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(LS(d+d_{v}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(LS(d+d_{v}))</annotation></semantics></math> up to projection costs, and is therefore linear in the number of image tokens. The routed sequence <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}^{\prime}=[\mathbf{x}^{\prime}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{x}^{\prime}_{L}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>L</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}^{\prime}=[\mathbf{x}^{\prime}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{x}^{\prime}_{L}]</annotation></semantics></math> has the same length as <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐗</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}</annotation></semantics></math> and can be passed directly to the selective SSM without changing the scan kernel.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4 </span>GraphScan as Selective-Scan Preconditioning</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">GraphScan can be viewed as a sparse, input-adaptive preconditioner for selective scan.
Let <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(\mathbf{X})\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(\mathbf{X})\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times L}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the sparse row-stochastic routing matrix induced by Eq. (<a href="#S3.E2" title="In 3.3 GraphScan: Local Semantic Routing ‣ 3 Method ‣ Can Graphs Help Vision SSMs See Better?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), with <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{ij}(\mathbf{X})=\alpha_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{ij}(\mathbf{X})=\alpha_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in\mathcal{S}_{r}(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in\mathcal{S}_{r}(i)</annotation></semantics></math> and zero otherwise.
Writing <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=W_{v}W_{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>o</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=W_{v}W_{o}</annotation></semantics></math>, Eq. (<a href="#S3.E3" title="In 3.3 GraphScan: Local Semantic Routing ‣ 3 Method ‣ Can Graphs Help Vision SSMs See Better?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) becomes</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}^{\prime}=\mathbf{X}+P(\mathbf{X})\mathbf{X}M,\qquad\mathbf{Y}=\mathrm{SSM}(\mathbf{X}^{\prime})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>SSM</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}^{\prime}=\mathbf{X}+P(\mathbf{X})\mathbf{X}M,\qquad\mathbf{Y}=\mathrm{SSM}(\mathbf{X}^{\prime}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Thus, GraphScan changes the sequence presented to the SSM while leaving the selective-scan recurrence and implementation unchanged.
The dependence of <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐗</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}</annotation></semantics></math> flows through the <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> projections inside the affinity computation in Eq. (<a href="#S3.E2" title="In 3.3 GraphScan: Local Semantic Routing ‣ 3 Method ‣ Can Graphs Help Vision SSMs See Better?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Parameter-modulated selective scan.</span>
The effect of Eq. (<a href="#S3.E4" title="In 3.4 GraphScan as Selective-Scan Preconditioning ‣ 3 Method ‣ Can Graphs Help Vision SSMs See Better?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) is not limited to changing token values.
Since selective SSMs generate <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\Delta}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝚫</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\Delta}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathbf{B}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐁</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathbf{B}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{c}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐜</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{c}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> from the current token, replacing <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}^{\prime}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}^{\prime}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> also changes the input-dependent parameters of the recurrence.
The routed recurrence is therefore</p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E5X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E5X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{H}^{\prime}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐇</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{H}^{\prime}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E5X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\bar{\mathbf{A}}^{\prime}_{t}\odot\mathbf{H}^{\prime}_{t-1}+\bar{\mathbf{B}}^{\prime}_{t}\odot(\mathbf{x}^{\prime}_{t})^{\uparrow_{s}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><mi>𝐇</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐁</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\bar{\mathbf{A}}^{\prime}_{t}\odot\mathbf{H}^{\prime}_{t-1}+\bar{\mathbf{B}}^{\prime}_{t}\odot(\mathbf{x}^{\prime}_{t})^{\uparrow_{s}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E5Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E5Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{y}^{\prime}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{y}^{\prime}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E5Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\left\langle\mathbf{H}^{\prime}_{t},(\mathbf{c}^{\prime}_{t})^{\uparrow_{D}}\right\rangle_{N_{s}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mo>⟨</mo><mi>𝐇</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐜</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>D</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo>⟩</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\left\langle\mathbf{H}^{\prime}_{t},(\mathbf{c}^{\prime}_{t})^{\uparrow_{D}}\right\rangle_{N_{s}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathbf{A}}^{\prime}_{t}=\bar{\mathbf{A}}_{t}(\mathbf{x}^{\prime}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathbf{A}}^{\prime}_{t}=\bar{\mathbf{A}}_{t}(\mathbf{x}^{\prime}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathbf{B}}^{\prime}_{t}=\bar{\mathbf{B}}_{t}(\mathbf{x}^{\prime}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐁</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐁</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathbf{B}}^{\prime}_{t}=\bar{\mathbf{B}}_{t}(\mathbf{x}^{\prime}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{c}^{\prime}_{t}=\mathbf{c}_{t}(\mathbf{x}^{\prime}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐜</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐜</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{c}^{\prime}_{t}=\mathbf{c}_{t}(\mathbf{x}^{\prime}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>.
This is the central reason for placing GraphScan immediately before the SSM: local semantic routing affects both the values written into the recurrent state and the token-dependent parameters that govern propagation and readout.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Exact routed-vs-base decomposition.</span>
Let</p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{m}_{t}=\mathbf{x}^{\prime}_{t}-\mathbf{x}_{t}=\sum_{j\in\mathcal{S}_{r}(t)}\alpha_{tj}\mathbf{x}_{j}M" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐦</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{m}_{t}=\mathbf{x}^{\prime}_{t}-\mathbf{x}_{t}=\sum_{j\in\mathcal{S}_{r}(t)}\alpha_{tj}\mathbf{x}_{j}M</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p3.2" class="ltx_p">denote the GraphScan message added to token <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>.
Define the exact parameter differences
<math id="S3.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\bar{\mathbf{A}}_{t}=\bar{\mathbf{A}}^{\prime}_{t}-\bar{\mathbf{A}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\bar{\mathbf{A}}_{t}=\bar{\mathbf{A}}^{\prime}_{t}-\bar{\mathbf{A}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S3.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\bar{\mathbf{B}}_{t}=\bar{\mathbf{B}}^{\prime}_{t}-\bar{\mathbf{B}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐁</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐁</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐁</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\bar{\mathbf{B}}_{t}=\bar{\mathbf{B}}^{\prime}_{t}-\bar{\mathbf{B}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, and
<math id="S3.SS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\mathbf{c}_{t}=\mathbf{c}^{\prime}_{t}-\mathbf{c}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐜</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐜</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐜</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\mathbf{c}_{t}=\mathbf{c}^{\prime}_{t}-\mathbf{c}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, where unprimed quantities are generated from the base token <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Comparing the routed recurrence in Eq. (<a href="#S3.E5" title="In 3.4 GraphScan as Selective-Scan Preconditioning ‣ 3 Method ‣ Can Graphs Help Vision SSMs See Better?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) with the base recurrence in Eq. (<a href="#S3.E1" title="In 3.1 Vision Selective Scan Preliminaries ‣ 3 Method ‣ Can Graphs Help Vision SSMs See Better?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) yields the exact decomposition</p>
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\delta\mathbf{H}_{t}=\sum_{i=1}^{t}\mathbf{G}^{\prime}_{t,i}\odot\left[\bar{\mathbf{B}}^{\prime}_{i}\odot\mathbf{m}_{i}^{\uparrow_{s}}+\delta\bar{\mathbf{B}}_{i}\odot\mathbf{x}_{i}^{\uparrow_{s}}+\delta\bar{\mathbf{A}}_{i}\odot\mathbf{H}_{i-1}\right]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><msub><mi>𝐇</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>t</mi></munderover><msubsup><mi>𝐆</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐁</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐦</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐁</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><msub><mi>𝐇</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\mathbf{H}_{t}=\sum_{i=1}^{t}\mathbf{G}^{\prime}_{t,i}\odot\left[\bar{\mathbf{B}}^{\prime}_{i}\odot\mathbf{m}_{i}^{\uparrow_{s}}+\delta\bar{\mathbf{B}}_{i}\odot\mathbf{x}_{i}^{\uparrow_{s}}+\delta\bar{\mathbf{A}}_{i}\odot\mathbf{H}_{i-1}\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p3.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\mathbf{H}_{t}=\mathbf{H}^{\prime}_{t}-\mathbf{H}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐇</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐇</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐇</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\mathbf{H}_{t}=\mathbf{H}^{\prime}_{t}-\mathbf{H}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S3.SS4.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{G}^{\prime}_{t,i}=\bigodot_{j=i+1}^{t}\bar{\mathbf{A}}^{\prime}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐆</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨀</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{G}^{\prime}_{t,i}=\bigodot_{j=i+1}^{t}\bar{\mathbf{A}}^{\prime}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is the routed survival factor.
The corresponding output difference is</p>
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\delta\mathbf{y}_{t}=\left\langle\delta\mathbf{H}_{t},(\mathbf{c}^{\prime}_{t})^{\uparrow_{D}}\right\rangle_{N_{s}}+\left\langle\mathbf{H}_{t},\delta\mathbf{c}_{t}^{\uparrow_{D}}\right\rangle_{N_{s}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mi>δ</mi><msub><mi>𝐇</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐜</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>D</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><msub><mi>𝐇</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><msubsup><mi>𝐜</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>D</mi></msub></mrow></msubsup><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\mathbf{y}_{t}=\left\langle\delta\mathbf{H}_{t},(\mathbf{c}^{\prime}_{t})^{\uparrow_{D}}\right\rangle_{N_{s}}+\left\langle\mathbf{H}_{t},\delta\mathbf{c}_{t}^{\uparrow_{D}}\right\rangle_{N_{s}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p3.4" class="ltx_p">Eq. (<a href="#S3.E6" title="In 3.4 GraphScan as Selective-Scan Preconditioning ‣ 3 Method ‣ Can Graphs Help Vision SSMs See Better?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) separates three hidden-state effects of GraphScan: direct routed value injection through <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\bar{\mathbf{B}}^{\prime}_{i}\odot\mathbf{m}_{i}^{\uparrow_{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐁</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><mi>𝐦</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathbf{B}}^{\prime}_{i}\odot\mathbf{m}_{i}^{\uparrow_{s}}</annotation></semantics></math>, write modulation through <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\bar{\mathbf{B}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐁</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\bar{\mathbf{B}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and transition modulation through <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\bar{\mathbf{A}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\bar{\mathbf{A}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Eq. (<a href="#S3.E7" title="In 3.4 GraphScan as Selective-Scan Preconditioning ‣ 3 Method ‣ Can Graphs Help Vision SSMs See Better?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>) further adds readout modulation through <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\mathbf{c}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐜</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\mathbf{c}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus, GraphScan does not merely add a local message before the SSM; it changes how the selective scan writes, retains, and reads visual information.
The full derivation is given in Appendix <a href="#A1.SS2" title="A.2 Exact Routed-vs-Base Decomposition ‣ Appendix A Analysis of GraphScan-Induced Selective Scan ‣ Can Graphs Help Vision SSMs See Better?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Local-global kernel view.</span>
The direct routed value path in Eq. (<a href="#S3.E6" title="In 3.4 GraphScan as Selective-Scan Preconditioning ‣ 3 Method ‣ Can Graphs Help Vision SSMs See Better?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) exposes the local-global structure induced by GraphScan.
Define the routed SSM input-to-output operator</p>
<table id="S3.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{K}^{\prime}_{t,i}(\mathbf{z})=\left\langle\mathbf{G}^{\prime}_{t,i}\odot\bar{\mathbf{B}}^{\prime}_{i}\odot\mathbf{z}^{\uparrow_{s}},(\mathbf{c}^{\prime}_{t})^{\uparrow_{D}}\right\rangle_{N_{s}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐆</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐁</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><msup><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐜</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>D</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}^{\prime}_{t,i}(\mathbf{z})=\left\langle\mathbf{G}^{\prime}_{t,i}\odot\bar{\mathbf{B}}^{\prime}_{i}\odot\mathbf{z}^{\uparrow_{s}},(\mathbf{c}^{\prime}_{t})^{\uparrow_{D}}\right\rangle_{N_{s}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p4.2" class="ltx_p">Using <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{m}_{i}=\sum_{j\in\mathcal{S}_{r}(i)}\alpha_{ij}\mathbf{x}_{j}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐦</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{m}_{i}=\sum_{j\in\mathcal{S}_{r}(i)}\alpha_{ij}\mathbf{x}_{j}M</annotation></semantics></math>, the direct routed value contribution becomes</p>
<table id="S3.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\delta\mathbf{y}^{\mathrm{route}}_{t}=\sum_{j=1}^{L}\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}i\leq t:\\
j\in\mathcal{S}_{r}(i)\end{subarray}}\alpha_{ij}\,\mathcal{K}^{\prime}_{t,i}(\mathbf{x}_{j}M)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><msubsup><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>route</mi></msubsup><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></munderover><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\mathbf{y}^{\mathrm{route}}_{t}=\sum_{j=1}^{L}\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}i\leq t:\\
j\in\mathcal{S}_{r}(i)\end{subarray}}\alpha_{ij}\,\mathcal{K}^{\prime}_{t,i}(\mathbf{x}_{j}M).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p4.3" class="ltx_p">A source patch <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is therefore weighted twice: first by a local semantic affinity <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> on the image lattice, and then by the global selective propagation encoded in <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}^{\prime}_{t,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}^{\prime}_{t,i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
GraphScan consequently creates a local semantic shortcut before the one-dimensional recurrence, allowing spatially nearby or semantically related patches to influence the selective scan without relying solely on their raster-order distance.
The corresponding derivation is given in Appendix <a href="#A1.SS3" title="A.3 Local-Global Kernel Induced by GraphScan ‣ Appendix A Analysis of GraphScan-Induced Selective Scan ‣ Can Graphs Help Vision SSMs See Better?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Further structural consequences.</span>
The preconditioner view yields three additional consequences detailed in App. <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Analysis of GraphScan-Induced Selective Scan ‣ Can Graphs Help Vision SSMs See Better?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>: a scan-distance attenuation bound that governs how a source patch reaches output <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> through a routed slot <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> in terms of <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t-i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t-i</annotation></semantics></math> rather than raster distance (App. <a href="#A1.SS4" title="A.4 Scan-Distance Shortcuts under Bounded Survival ‣ Appendix A Analysis of GraphScan-Induced Selective Scan ‣ Can Graphs Help Vision SSMs See Better?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.4</span></a>); a modified finite-horizon input-to-state map with cross-token directions induced by the sparse routing matrix <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(\mathbf{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(\mathbf{X})</annotation></semantics></math> (App. <a href="#A1.SS5" title="A.5 Reachability View from State-Space Systems ‣ Appendix A Analysis of GraphScan-Induced Selective Scan ‣ Can Graphs Help Vision SSMs See Better?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.5</span></a>); and baseline containment—setting <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{o}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{o}=0</annotation></semantics></math> recovers <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}^{\prime}=\mathbf{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐗</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}^{\prime}=\mathbf{X}</annotation></semantics></math> (App. <a href="#A1.SS6" title="A.6 Baseline Containment ‣ Appendix A Analysis of GraphScan-Induced Selective Scan ‣ Can Graphs Help Vision SSMs See Better?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.6</span></a>). GraphScan therefore augments the backbone’s representational family while preserving the original Vision Mamba model as a special case.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Orthogonality to SSM cores.</span>
GraphScan is an input-side operator that leaves the discretization rule, selective scan recurrence, SSD formulation, and hidden-state update untouched, so it is complementary to recurrence-side improvements such as SSD, non-causal SSD, and newer Mamba variants <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>, <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>. We instantiate it on the Mamba-1/S6 Vision Mamba interface to isolate the effect of scanning and enable direct comparison with existing scan mechanisms.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.5 </span>GraphScan-Mamba Architecture</h3>

<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2605.11300v1/architecture.png" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:429/147;" width="429" height="147" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span><span id="S3.F3.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GraphScan-Mamba architecture.</span> <em id="S3.F3.7" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Top:</em> four-stage hierarchical backbone with an overlapping convolutional stem to <math id="S3.F3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/4</annotation></semantics></math> resolution and stage-wise downsampling, with stage-wise graph radii <math id="S3.F3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=(1,1,2,3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=(1,1,2,3)</annotation></semantics></math> yielding <math id="S3.F3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3{\times}3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3{\times}3</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.F3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3{\times}3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3{\times}3</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.F3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5{\times}5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5{\times}5</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.F3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7{\times}7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7{\times}7</annotation></semantics></math> local neighborhoods. <em id="S3.F3.8" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Middle:</em> each of <math id="S3.F3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> blocks combines a depthwise positional branch, a GraphScan-SSM token-mixing branch, and a ConvFFN branch. <em id="S3.F3.9" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Bottom:</em> inside GSSM, features pass through <math id="S3.F3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1{\times}1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1{\times}1</annotation></semantics></math> projection, <math id="S3.F3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3{\times}3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3{\times}3</annotation></semantics></math> depthwise conv, SiLU, GraphScan, selective SSM, normalization, and output projection—a fixed-local <math id="S3.F3.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math> learned-local <math id="S3.F3.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math> global progression.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We integrate GraphScan into a hierarchical Vision Mamba backbone, <span id="S3.SS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GraphScan-Mamba</span>, following the standard four-stage design used by modern vision backbones <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>, <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>, <a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">68</a>, <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>: an overlapping convolutional stem produces a <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/4</annotation></semantics></math>-resolution feature map, and subsequent stages progressively downsample spatial resolution while increasing channel dimension. This architectural template is deliberately conventional; our goal is to isolate the effect of replacing geometric pre-SSM routing with graph-induced semantic routing. Fig. <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3.5 GraphScan-Mamba Architecture ‣ 3 Method ‣ Can Graphs Help Vision SSMs See Better?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> summarizes the full backbone, the repeated GraphScan block, and the GSSM token mixer where GraphScan is inserted before the selective SSM.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Block structure.</span>
Each block has three residual components applied in sequence: <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\leftarrow X+\mathrm{DWConv}_{3\times 3}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>DWConv</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\leftarrow X+\mathrm{DWConv}_{3\times 3}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\leftarrow X+\mathrm{GSSM}(\mathrm{LN}(X))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>GSSM</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>LN</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\leftarrow X+\mathrm{GSSM}(\mathrm{LN}(X))</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\leftarrow X+\mathrm{ConvFFN}(\mathrm{BN}(X))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ConvFFN</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>BN</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\leftarrow X+\mathrm{ConvFFN}(\mathrm{BN}(X))</annotation></semantics></math> (omitting drop path and layer-scale factors). The first is a residual depthwise convolution that injects local positional bias <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>, fused at implementation time into a single convolution with an identity-offset kernel. <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{ConvFFN}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ConvFFN</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{ConvFFN}</annotation></semantics></math> stacks a <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1{\times}1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1{\times}1</annotation></semantics></math> pointwise convolution, a <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3{\times}3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3{\times}3</annotation></semantics></math> depthwise convolution, and a final <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1{\times}1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1{\times}1</annotation></semantics></math> pointwise convolution <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">63</a>, <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GraphScan-SSM token mixer.</span>
GraphScan is inserted between the input stage and the selective SSM: <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{X}=\mathrm{SiLU}(\mathrm{DWConv}_{3\times 3}(\mathrm{Proj}_{\mathrm{in}}(X)))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>SiLU</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>DWConv</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Proj</mi><mi>in</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{X}=\mathrm{SiLU}(\mathrm{DWConv}_{3\times 3}(\mathrm{Proj}_{\mathrm{in}}(X)))</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\mathrm{gs}}=\mathrm{GraphScan}(\widetilde{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>gs</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>GraphScan</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\mathrm{gs}}=\mathrm{GraphScan}(\widetilde{X})</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{GSSM}(X)=\mathrm{Proj}_{\mathrm{out}}(\mathrm{LN}(\mathrm{SSM}(X^{\mathrm{gs}})))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>GSSM</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Proj</mi><mi>out</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>LN</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>SSM</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>gs</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{GSSM}(X)=\mathrm{Proj}_{\mathrm{out}}(\mathrm{LN}(\mathrm{SSM}(X^{\mathrm{gs}})))</annotation></semantics></math>. Each token mixer therefore follows a <em id="S3.SS5.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">fixed-local</em> (depthwise convolution) <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math> <em id="S3.SS5.p3.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">learned-local</em> (GraphScan) <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math> <em id="S3.SS5.p3.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">global</em> (selective SSM) progression.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS5.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS5.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Stage-wise graph radius.</span>
The graph radius <math id="S3.SS5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> grows with stage depth: smaller windows at high resolution, where local mixing suffices, and larger windows in low-resolution late stages, where the receptive field needs to become effectively global. At the final stage, the GraphScan neighborhood spans the entire feature map while remaining linear in the spatial budget. Exact radii, channel widths, depths, and training configurations are reported in Sec. <a href="#S4" title="4 Experiments ‣ Can Graphs Help Vision SSMs See Better?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experiments</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate GraphScan-Mamba on three standard recognition benchmarks: ImageNet-1K classification <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>, COCO object detection and instance segmentation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite> with Mask R-CNN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>, and ADE20K semantic segmentation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">76</a>]</cite> with UperNet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">69</a>]</cite>. Across all three tasks, GraphScan-Mamba achieves state-of-the-art accuracy among Vision SSM backbones at comparable parameter and FLOP budgets. We also ablate pre-SSM placement, graph radius, relative positional bias, and head count; per-block attention and effective-routing visualizations of the trained backbone are reported in App. <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Visualizations ‣ Can Graphs Help Vision SSMs See Better?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Architecture variants.</span>
We instantiate three model sizes (Tiny, Small, Base), following the conventional channel-and-depth scaling axes used by hierarchical Vision Mamba backbones <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>. The macro topology, MLP ratios, and graph radii are shared across variants; only channel widths and stage depths vary. Radii follow <math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=(1,1,2,3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=(1,1,2,3)</annotation></semantics></math>, yielding <math id="S4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3{\times}3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3{\times}3</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3{\times}3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3{\times}3</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5{\times}5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5{\times}5</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7{\times}7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7{\times}7</annotation></semantics></math> neighborhoods at stages 1–4; the stage-4 neighborhood spans the full <math id="S4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7{\times}7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7{\times}7</annotation></semantics></math> feature map. Per-variant channel widths, depths, MLP ratios, parameters, and FLOPs are reported in App. <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Architecture Variants ‣ Can Graphs Help Vision SSMs See Better?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Image Classification on ImageNet-1K</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.2" class="ltx_logical-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_top" style="width:172.5pt;">
        
<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Setup.</span>
We follow the standard DeiT-style training recipe <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">55</a>]</cite> adopted across recent Vision Mamba works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>. All variants are trained for 300 epochs at <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="224^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>224</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">224^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> resolution using AdamW with a cosine learning-rate schedule, 20-epoch linear warmup, base learning rate <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1{\times}10^{-3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1{\times}10^{-3}</annotation></semantics></math>, weight decay <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5{\times}10^{-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5{\times}10^{-2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and batch size 1024. Augmentation and regularization include RandAugment, Repeated Augmentation, Mixup, CutMix, Random Erasing, label smoothing, and stochastic depth, with the maximum stochastic-depth rate linearly interpolated to <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{0.2,0.3,0.5\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0.2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{0.2,0.3,0.5\}</annotation></semantics></math> for T/S/B. Center cropping is applied at evaluation. FLOPs and parameters are reported at <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="224^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>224</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">224^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
        
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Results.</span>
As shown in Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.1 Image Classification on ImageNet-1K ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Can Graphs Help Vision SSMs See Better?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, GraphScan-Mamba consistently outperforms ConvNet, Transformer, and Vision SSM backbones at every parameter scale. GraphScan-Mamba-B reaches <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="86.5\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>86.5</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">86.5\%</annotation></semantics></math> top-1 accuracy, <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+1.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1.2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+1.2</annotation></semantics></math> over the strongest Vision SSM at this scale (Spatial-Mamba-B at <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="85.3\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>85.3</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">85.3\%</annotation></semantics></math>) and <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+2.6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2.6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+2.6</annotation></semantics></math> over VMamba-B at <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="83.9\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>83.9</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">83.9\%</annotation></semantics></math>. At the Small scale, GraphScan-Mamba-S obtains <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="85.7\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>85.7</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">85.7\%</annotation></semantics></math>, exceeding DAMamba-S (<math id="S4.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="84.8\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>84.8</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">84.8\%</annotation></semantics></math>) by <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+0.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+0.9</annotation></semantics></math> and VMamba-S (<math id="S4.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="83.6\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>83.6</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">83.6\%</annotation></semantics></math>) by <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+2.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+2.1</annotation></semantics></math>. At Tiny, GraphScan-Mamba-T attains <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="84.4\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>84.4</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">84.4\%</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+0.6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>0.6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+0.6</annotation></semantics></math> above the strongest Vision SSM at this scale (DAMamba-T at <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="83.8\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>83.8</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">83.8\%</annotation></semantics></math>) and <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+1.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1.9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+1.9</annotation></semantics></math> above VMamba-T at <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="82.5\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>82.5</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">82.5\%</annotation></semantics></math>. The lead is preserved against the strongest ConvNets and Transformers at matched parameter budgets, including ConvNeXt-B (<math id="S4.SS1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="83.8\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>83.8</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">83.8\%</annotation></semantics></math>), Swin-B (<math id="S4.SS1.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="83.5\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>83.5</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">83.5\%</annotation></semantics></math>), and NAT-B (<math id="S4.SS1.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="84.3\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>84.3</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">84.3\%</annotation></semantics></math>). These results demonstrate that injecting a local semantic routing layer before the selective scan is an effective lever for surpassing strong ConvNet and Transformer backbones while preserving the linear scaling of state-space models.</p>
</div>
      
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.3" class="ltx_logical-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_top" style="width:165.6pt;">
        
<figure id="S4.T2" class="ltx_table ltx_align_center">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span><span id="S4.T2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Image classification on ImageNet-1K.</span> All entries use <math id="S4.T2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="224^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>224</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">224^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> input. “Type”: ConvNet (C), Transformer (T), State-Space Model (S), or Graph Neural Network (G). Best result per parameter regime is in <span id="S4.T2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">bold</span>.</figcaption>
</figure>
        
